Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Warm period storybook live-action; soft natural light; toys and animals share one continuous medium

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A Edwardian nursery, pale winter light. A child's stocking bulges on the bedpost. At the top, wedged charmingly, sits the VELVETEEN RABBIT: fat and bunchy, spotted brown-and-white velveteen, real thread whiskers, ears lined with pink sateen, a sprig of holly between his paws.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

Below him in the stocking: nuts, oranges, a toy engine, chocolate almonds, a clockwork mouse.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Rabbit was quite the best of all.

THE BOY, about seven, fair hair, nightshirt, pulls the Rabbit free and hugs him. For a bright stretch of morning the Boy loves him wholly.

Then aunts and uncles fill the doorway. Tissue paper rustles. Parcels tear open. In the excitement of new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit is set aside on the nursery floor, forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Weeks later. The Rabbit lives in the toy cupboard and on the floor. Mechanical toys gleam and swagger. A MODEL BOAT, paint worn, sits aloof. TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, holds his chin high. The Rabbit, shy, made only of velveteen, looks very small among them.

Near the fender lies the SKIN HORSE: ancient, brown coat bald in patches, seams showing, tail hairs sparse. Kind eyes. He alone is gentle with the Rabbit.

They lie side by side before Nana's tidy-up.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, longing and a little afraid of shabbiness.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Bedtime. THE BOY cannot find his china dog. NANA, starched apron, practical and brisk, is in a hurry. The toy cupboard door stands open. She swoops.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Rabbit out by one ear and puts him into the Boy's arms.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Boy's bed, night-light burning on the mantelpiece. The Boy hugs the Rabbit very tight, rolls onto him, pushes him under the pillow. The Rabbit endures, missing the moonlight nursery and the Skin Horse.

Nights pass in a gentle rhythm. The Boy whispers, makes tunnels under the bedclothes.

THE BOY
(soft, to the Rabbit)
Burrows. Like the real rabbits live in.

When Nana has gone to supper they play splendid games in whispers. When the Boy sleeps, the Rabbit snuggles under his warm chin, the Boy's hands clasped round him all night long.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy.

CLOSE ON the Rabbit: velveteen fur growing shabbier, tail coming unsewn, pink rubbed off his nose where the Boy has kissed him. He never notices.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. Long days. The Boy wheels the Rabbit in a barrow, picnics on the grass, builds fairy huts under the raspberry canes. Once the Rabbit is left on the lawn until dusk, wet with dew, earthy from flower-bed burrows. Nana finds him by candlelight and grumbles as she rubs him with her apron.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Nana carries the damp Rabbit in. The Boy sits up, arms out.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit hears. Stillness. Joy.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last. The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was a toy no longer. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

That night love stirs in his sawdust heart. Into his boot-button eyes, long without polish, comes a look of wisdom and beauty.

Morning. Nana picks him up, studies his face.

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

A wonderful summer. Long June evening. Near the house, a wood. The Boy makes the Rabbit a little nest in the bracken, then wanders off among the trees.

The Rabbit lies alone, watching ants run between his velvet paws.

Two WILD RABBITS creep from the tall bracken: furry, brand-new, seams invisible, bodies changing shape as they move—long and thin, then fat and bunchy. They pad close, noses twitching. The Velveteen Rabbit stares for clockwork. There is none.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

The Wild Rabbit hops sideways, stands on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

Dread. The Velveteen Rabbit has no hind legs—the back of him is all one piece, like a pincushion. He sits still.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The Wild Rabbit stretches his neck and looks.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

He laughs. The second wild rabbit joins the laughter.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

The Wild Rabbit whirls and dances until the little Rabbit is dizzy.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

All the while a tickly longing runs through him—to jump as they do. The Wild Rabbit comes so close his whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear—then wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, jumps backward.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. The Boy runs past. A stamp of feet, a flash of white tails—the wild rabbits vanish.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

No answer. Only ants, and bracken swaying. The Velveteen Rabbit is alone until the Boy carries him home.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY

Weeks pass. The Rabbit is very old and shabby: whiskers loved off, ear linings grey, brown spots faded, shape half lost. To the Boy he is always beautiful.

Then—the Boy is ill. Face flushed. He talks in his sleep. His little body burns so hot it scorches the Rabbit. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night. The Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes, never stirring, afraid someone will take him away.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He knew that the Boy needed him.

He snuggles patiently, whispers planned garden games into the Boy's ear. The fever turns. The Boy sits up with picture-books, the Rabbit close at his side. One bright morning they let the Boy get up and dress.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY

Windows wide open. Sunny. The Boy has been carried out to the balcony in a shawl. The little Rabbit lies tangled in the bedclothes, only his head peeping out.

Voices in the room: the seaside tomorrow. The room to be disinfected. Books and toys played with in bed must be burnt.

The Rabbit's hope flares.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hurrah! thought the little Rabbit. To-morrow we shall go to the seaside!

Nana's eye falls on him.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

The DOCTOR, bag in hand, glances once.

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs!—Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - DAY

The Rabbit is stuffed into a sack with old picture-books and rubbish, carried to the end of the garden behind the fowl-house—a fine place for a bonfire. The gardener is busy with potatoes and peas. He will burn the lot in the morning. The sack is left untied.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

While the Boy sleeps in another room with a new white-plush bunny, the little Rabbit wriggles his head through the sack opening. He shivers. His coat is threadbare. Nearby, the raspberry thicket stands tall—the shadow of old games.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of those long sunlit hours in the garden—how happy they were—and a great sadness came over him.

Memory touches his face: fairy huts, ants on his paws, the day he knew he was Real, the wise Skin Horse.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A tear—a real tear—trickles down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

Where the tear falls, a flower pushes from the earth: slender emerald leaves, a blossom like a golden cup. The Rabbit forgets to cry and watches. The blossom opens. Out steps the NURSERY FAIRY—dress of pearl and dewdrops, flowers at her neck and in her hair, face like the most perfect flower.

She gathers him up and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

NURSERY FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

He looks up—her face almost remembered.

NURSERY FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

She holds him close and flies with him into the wood.

EXT. WOOD GLADE - NIGHT

Moon risen. Bracken fronds shine like frosted silver. In the open glade wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass. They stop and stand in a ring, staring at the Fairy.

NURSERY FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit again and sets him on the grass.

NURSERY FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

He sits frozen, remembering his hind legs, ashamed to be seen all in one piece. He does not know the last kiss changed him altogether.

Something tickles his nose. Without thinking he lifts a hind toe to scratch—

He has hind legs. Brown fur, soft and shiny, instead of dingy velveteen. Ears that twitch by themselves. Whiskers so long they brush the grass.

He gives one leap. Joy breaks through him. He springs about the turf, jumps sideways, whirls as the others do—until, breathless, he looks for the Fairy. She is gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Autumn and winter have passed. Spring again—warm and sunny. The Boy plays in the wood behind the house.

Two rabbits creep from the bracken and peep at him. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted, and the spots still show through. About his soft nose and round black eyes, something familiar.

THE BOY
(to himself)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit watches the child who first helped him to be Real. The Boy never knows. Leaves stir. The rabbits slip back into the bracken.

FADE OUT.

THE END
